"""Explicit region, adaptation and forest-management content."""
from assemble_topic import assemble
FACTS={
'01':['Rainforest, savannah and tropical deserts have distinct broad distributions modified by climate, relief and land use.','Vegetation boundaries can be gradual and need not follow country borders or one exact latitude.'],
'01-01':['Major tropical rainforest examples occur in the Amazon and Congo basins and parts of Southeast Asia around low latitudes.','Separate forest blocks should not be described as one continuous band across oceans and drier areas.'],
'01-02':['Savannah occurs widely in tropical Africa, parts of South America and northern Australia, with seasonal water conditions.','Savannah structure varies with rainfall, soil, fire, grazing and management rather than uniform tree spacing.'],
'01-03':['Tropical deserts commonly occur in subtropical dry settings, including Sahara, Arabia, Namib and Australian examples.','Desert aridity is not synonymous with sand-dune cover, and cold coastal influence can modify temperature.'],
'02':['An adaptation explanation connects a plant feature to a relevant water, light, support or disturbance constraint.','Features vary among species and should not be transferred indiscriminately from rainforest to desert.'],
'02-01':['Layered rainforest vegetation creates light differences, while tall growth, support roots, climbers and leaf-drainage features have context-specific functions.','Evergreen forest appearance does not mean each leaf persists indefinitely.'],
'02-02':['Savannah vegetation includes grasses and scattered woody plants responding to wet–dry seasons and other controls.','Dry-season leaf shedding, suitable root systems and regrowth strategies can support survival, without making all fire or grazing harmless.'],
'02-03':['Desert plants can use water conservation, storage, deep or spreading roots, and short life cycles as differing strategies.','Reduced leaves and waxy surfaces can reduce water loss, while water-storing tissues address intervals between supplies.'],
'03':['Temperature, rainfall, wind, cloud and humidity are distinct but related climate characteristics.','A temperature/rainfall graph alone cannot directly supply exact wind directions, cloud cover or humidity values.'],
'03-01':['Equatorial rainforest models commonly combine year-round warmth, small annual temperature range and abundant rain.','Actual forest regions vary seasonally and geographically, so the model does not establish equal rain every month.'],
'03-02':['Savannah models commonly combine warm conditions with a marked wet–dry seasonal contrast.','The actual months and number of rainfall peaks must follow the site graph rather than one assumed pattern.'],
'03-03':['Tropical deserts are water-limited with low irregular rainfall, while inland temperature ranges can be large under suitable conditions.','Namib coastal fog and cool marine influence show why a desert need not match every inland hot-desert temperature feature.'],
'04':['Annual rainfall adds complete comparable monthly totals; annual temperature range subtracts lowest monthly mean from highest.','Supplied series A totals 2,000 mm with a 1°C temperature range, B 500 mm with 9°C, and C 20 mm with 13°C.'],
'05':['Forest conversion and degradation can result from agriculture, access roads, extraction and other land uses with linked incentives.','Desertification is degradation in drylands involving climatic variation and/or human activities, not simply an existing desert expanding.'],
'05-01':['NASA Amazon sequences document road-access and crop/pasture conversion pathways without implying one cause for the entire basin.','Clearance can affect habitats, soil protection, water exchange and carbon through distinct mechanisms.'],
'05-02':['Dryland cover loss and soil degradation can reduce productivity without a dune entering the site.','Drought, natural desert conditions and desertification are different concepts requiring time and baseline evidence.'],
'06':['Agroforestry, reforestation and sustainable harvesting have different functions and conditions.','A named scheme or planting input alone does not establish a sustained environmental or livelihood outcome.'],
'06-01':['Agroforestry integrates trees with crops and/or livestock, with benefits and competition depending on design.','Spacing, species, water and management affect whether the intended mixed-use objective is met.'],
'06-02':['Reforestation restores forest on previously forested land through suitable planting or regeneration methods.','Seedlings planted, trees surviving and restored ecosystem functions are different outcomes.'],
'06-03':['Harvesting needs to remain compatible with regeneration and stated forest functions over time.','In a simplified balance with removal 100 units and growth 60, stock falls 40, so maintained supply is not supported.']}
CARDS={
'001':('Name one major equatorial rainforest basin in Africa.','The Congo Basin.',['1 mark for the name Congo Basin.']),
'002':('Describe the rainforest, savannah and tropical-desert distribution patterns in the supplied original world map, using its legend and latitude references.','Rainforest forms separate low-latitude blocks such as Amazon, Congo and Southeast Asia. Savannah occupies seasonal tropical regions in several continents. Tropical deserts include subtropical dry-belt and coastal/interior examples, not one continuous uniform band.',['1 mark for the rainforest-block pattern; 1 for savannah evidence from the map; 1 for the mapped desert-location contrast.']),
'003':('Describe the rainforest pattern in this map record: shaded blocks cover Amazon, Congo and parts of Southeast Asia near low latitudes, with oceans and drier regions between.','The distribution has separate continental blocks near the Equator. The named examples occur across more than one continent. It is not a continuous forest belt across the intervening oceans or dry areas.',['1 mark for separate low-latitude pattern; 1 for named-block evidence; 1 for the interrupted-distribution interpretation.']),
'004':('Describe the savannah pattern: the resource maps seasonal tropical grassland across parts of Africa, South America and northern Australia, with varying tree cover.','Savannah occurs in several tropical continental regions. Its tree cover varies rather than forming a uniform closed canopy. The mapped seasonal-grassland pattern should not be extended to each entire country.',['1 mark for the multicontinental feature; 1 for seasonal/variable-cover evidence; 1 for the qualified regional extent.']),
'005':('Describe the tropical-desert distribution in a resource marking Sahara, Arabia, Namib and Australian interior examples, with the Namib beside cold coastal water.','The examples include subtropical dry areas in both hemispheres. They occur inland and along a cold-influenced coast. The pattern cannot be reduced to all deserts being inland sand dunes.',['1 mark for the hemispheric/dry-belt pattern; 1 for named coastal/interior evidence; 1 for the correct extent of the interpretation.']),
'006':('Explain how two contrasting plant features can address different environmental constraints: rainforest drip tips and desert reduced leaves with a waxy surface.','Drip tips help water drain from leaves in a moist setting. Reduced leaves reduce exposed transpiring area and a waxy surface can limit water loss in water-limited conditions. The feature–function links differ with the constraint.',['1 mark for a relevant moisture constraint; 1 for the drip-tip drainage link; 1 for the desert water-loss factor; 1 for the reduced-area/waxy-surface effect.']),
'007':('Describe the adaptation contrasts: A has rainforest drip tips and a supported tall trunk; B sheds leaves in a dry season; C has reduced leaves and a water-storing stem.','A combines drainage and support features in a moist forest context. B reduces seasonal transpiring surface. C combines conservation and storage for water-limited intervals.',['1 mark for A’s feature–context relationship; 1 for B’s seasonal evidence; 1 for C’s storage/conservation interpretation.']),
'008':('Describe the rainforest interpretation: a profile shows canopy layers, shaded lower plants, tall trunks with buttress supports and leaves with drip tips.','The layered structure creates differing light conditions. Buttress forms support tall growth, and drip tips help shed water. The supplied features support a moist layered-forest interpretation, not a desert-water-storage explanation.',['1 mark for the light-layer pattern; 1 for root/support evidence; 1 for the drainage/context relationship.']),
'009':('Describe the savannah evidence: grasses grow during wet months, some trees lose leaves in the dry season, and scattered woody cover remains.','Vegetation responds to seasonal water availability. Leaf loss can reduce dry-period water use. Grass and scattered trees fit a savannah structure rather than a continuous closed rainforest canopy.',['1 mark for the seasonal-growth pattern; 1 for leaf-loss evidence; 1 for the structural interpretation.']),
'010':('Describe the desert adaptation evidence: a plant has a thick water-storing stem, reduced leaves and a waxy surface; the resource records long intervals between water supplies.','Storage buffers supply gaps. Reduced leaves and waxy surfaces can limit water loss. The combined features fit the stated water limitation rather than a drip-tip drainage function.',['1 mark for the storage/supply relationship; 1 for the conservation-feature evidence; 1 for the correct environmental interpretation.']),
'011':('Explain how seasonal water availability can help produce savannah vegetation rather than the moist evergreen rainforest model.','A marked dry season limits available water and growth. Some trees shed leaves and grasses become dormant or dry, reducing demand or waiting for renewed supply. Wetter periods permit growth, supporting a seasonal grass-and-tree pattern.',['1 mark for seasonal shortage as a factor; 1 for the water-to-growth link; 1 for a relevant plant response; 1 for its seasonal vegetation effect.']),
'012':('Describe the climate contrasts: A has warm monthly means with rain throughout the year; B has a marked wet/dry season; C has very little irregular rain and a cold-influenced coastal option.','A supports a rainforest model and B seasonal savannah. C supports water-limited desert conditions, with coastal influence capable of moderating temperature and supporting fog. Precise wind or humidity values need their own data.',['1 mark for A’s warm/wet pattern; 1 for B’s seasonal evidence; 1 for the qualified C interpretation.']),
'013':('Describe supplied series A: monthly mean temperature 26–27°C, rain in every month and annual total 2,000 mm.','Temperature stays warm with a 1°C annual range. Rain is abundant across the year in this example. The pattern is consistent with an equatorial rainforest model but does not establish a unique real location.',['1 mark for the small warm-range feature; 1 for rainfall evidence; 1 for the limited regional classification.']),
'014':('Describe supplied series B: 500 mm annual rain, several zero-rain winter months, wetter early/late calendar months, monthly mean temperature 18–27°C.','It has marked Southern Hemisphere summer-wet seasonality and a 9°C annual temperature range. The wet/dry contrast is consistent with a savannah model. Exact humidity and wind cannot be read from those two variables alone.',['1 mark for seasonal rainfall pattern; 1 for the temperature-range evidence; 1 for a qualified savannah interpretation.']),
'015':('Describe the desert contrast: original inland series C has 20 mm annual rain and 17–30°C monthly means, while a separate Namib coastal record includes fog and cool marine influence.','Both can be water-limited desert settings despite different temperatures. C has a 13°C annual range; the coast has a marine modification and fog possibility. Fog does not by itself imply abundant rainfall.',['1 mark for shared low-water feature; 1 for C/coastal evidence contrast; 1 for the fog/rain distinction.']),
'016':('Calculate annual rainfall for original monthly series B: 100,90,80,40,10,0,0,0,5,25,60,90 mm.','The twelve-month total is 500 mm.',['1 mark for the method of adding all twelve monthly totals; 1 for the correct answer 500; 1 for rainfall depth unit mm, rather than a monthly mean.']),
'017':('Describe and compare the supplied original climate graphs: A totals 2,000 mm with 26–27°C monthly means; B 500 mm with 18–27°C and a summer-wet season; C 20 mm with 17–30°C.','A has high year-round rainfall and small range, B marked rain seasonality and a larger range, and C very low rain with the largest range of the three. The contrasts support rainforest, savannah and inland-desert models respectively.',['1 mark for A’s graph pattern; 1 for B’s seasonal/value evidence; 1 for C’s aridity/range contrast and correct comparative interpretation.']),
'018':('Explain a road-access pathway to forest clearance and one linked environmental consequence.','A road can make extraction and new land uses accessible. Subsequent crop or pasture conversion can remove forest cover. Habitat loss or exposed-soil erosion then follows through the relevant local mechanism.',['1 mark for access as a factor; 1 for its link to extraction/conversion; 1 for a specific consequence; 1 for explaining the cover-to-habitat/soil effect.']),
'019':('Describe what two images support: the later image has less green cover, but dates are in different seasons and classification methods differ.','The mapped appearance changes, but seasonal response and method differences confound the comparison. Persistent clearance or degradation is not established from colour change alone. Comparable dates/categories and ground evidence would strengthen interpretation.',['1 mark for the changed-cover appearance pattern; 1 for the season/method evidence; 1 for the justified limit and targeted evidence need.']),
'020':('Name one land use documented as a forest-conversion pathway in Amazon examples.','Cattle pasture; crop cultivation is also relevant.',['1 mark for the name of a relevant land use such as pasture or crops.']),
'021':('Name the term for degradation of dryland productivity involving climatic variation and/or human activities, rather than merely a dune moving.','Desertification.',['1 mark for the name desertification.']),
'022':('Name the management system combining trees with crops and/or livestock.','Agroforestry.',['1 mark for the name agroforestry.']),
'023':('How far do you agree that calling a forest scheme sustainable proves it maintains forest functions? It reports planting totals and a cutting rule, but no survival, regeneration or compliance observations. Judge maintained recovery and use.','Disagree. A name and stated inputs do not establish survival or regeneration, while a rule does not prove compliance. Monitoring recovery, actual extraction and relevant habitat/soil functions is needed. The scheme may help, but the claimed outcome is not demonstrated by those records alone.',['Up to 4 C marks for judging planting input, missing survival/regeneration evidence, compliance and the stated functions.','1 C mark for a supported decision distinguishing a plan from observed performance.']),
'024':('How far do you agree that agroforestry automatically meets the crop-and-tree objective? The proposal has suitable spacing and useful species, but no plan for water competition or pruning through dry periods.','Partly agree with its potential, not automatic success. Suitable species and spacing support mixed use, while unmanaged water competition and canopy growth can reduce the crop benefit. A workable water/pruning plan and monitoring are needed before both objectives are established.',['Up to 4 C marks for the design evidence, benefit mechanism and conditional water/pruning limitation.','1 C mark for a supported judgement against the combined-use criterion.']),
'025':('How far do you agree that 1,000 seedlings planted proves a restored forest? After the first dry season only 300 remain alive and no habitat-structure record is available. Judge established cover and ecosystem recovery.','Disagree. The planting count is an input; survival of 300 shows substantial loss and does not establish mature forest structure. Suitable aftercare and longer monitoring are needed for cover and wider functions. Some surviving growth is a benefit, but full restoration is not demonstrated.',['Up to 4 C marks for interpreting the planting/survival evidence, time scale and missing functional/structural information.','1 C mark for a supported decision distinguishing seedlings from restored forest.']),
'026':('How far do you agree that this selective harvest maintains stock? In a simplified interval, 100 units are removed, 60 regrow and other stock changes are stated absent. Judge the recorded wood balance and limits.','Disagree for maintained stock: removal exceeds growth by 40 units. The selective label does not change that balance. Reducing removal and checking regeneration are relevant, while habitat or species functions would need additional evidence beyond the volume balance.',['Up to 4 C marks for the 100/60 evidence, 40-unit decline, recovery implication and broader-function limitation.','1 C mark for a supported decision rejecting maintained stock for the stated interval.']),
'027':('Explain the error in applying rainforest drip tips and buttress roots as a generic desert water-storage answer. Compare a moist-forest drainage/support feature with a waxy water-storing stem in scarce-water conditions.','The confusion transfers features across different constraints. Drip tips shed water and buttresses support tall growth; a waxy succulent stem can conserve/store water between scarce supplies. The test is matching the observed feature to its actual function and setting.',['1 mark for the region/function confusion; 1 for the correct feature–function distinction applied as a discriminating test.'])}
TASKS=[
{'objectives':['01','02','04'],'prompt':'(a) Name a major African equatorial rainforest basin. [1]\n(b) Describe three different plant features and their relevant environmental functions across the studied regions. [3]\n(c) Name the region type with grass and scattered woody vegetation under seasonal water supply. [1]\n(d) Describe the rainfall and temperature-range contrast between original A (2,000 mm;26–27°C) and C (20 mm;17–30°C). [2]','answer':'(a) Congo Basin. (b) Examples include drip-tip drainage, root/trunk support in a moist tall forest, dry-season leaf loss reducing water use, reduced leaves/wax limiting loss or storage buffering supply gaps. (c) Savannah. (d) A is much wetter with a 1°C range, while C is far drier with a 13°C range.','guide':['(a) 1 A mark for Congo Basin.','(b) 3 A marks for three distinct relevant feature/function point descriptions; do not reward the same water-loss idea repeatedly.','(c) 1 A mark for savannah.','(d) 2 B marks for the paired rainfall evidence and the paired temperature-range interpretation.'],'split':{'A':5,'B':2},'parts':[('a','Name',1,'A'),('b','Describe',3,'A'),('c','Name',1,'A'),('d','Describe',2,'B')]},
{'objectives':['03','04','05'],'prompt':'Original climate series A has rain every month totalling 2,000 mm and monthly mean temperature 26–27°C. B totals 500 mm with a marked summer-wet/dry-winter pattern and 18–27°C means. C totals 20 mm and 17–30°C means.\n(a) Name the likely broad vegetation model supported by A. [1]\n(b) Describe three contrasts across the series using the stated values. [3]\n(c) Calculate B’s annual temperature range and give its unit. [2]\n(d) What judgement is supported about these climate data alone proving human-caused desertification at C? [1]','answer':'(a) Equatorial/tropical rainforest. (b) A is wetter and has the smallest temperature range; B has marked rainfall seasonality; C is much drier with the greatest temperature range. (c) 27−18 = 9°C. (d) Human-caused degradation is not established: a dry climate record alone does not show land-condition decline or its cause.','guide':['(a) 1 A mark for rainforest.','(b) 3 B marks for three distinct comparative patterns supported by the resource evidence.','(c) 2 B marks for correct subtraction/result and °C unit.','(d) 1 C mark for a supported judgement distinguishing climate aridity from evidenced degradation/attribution.'],'split':{'A':1,'B':5,'C':1},'parts':[('a','Name',1,'A'),('b','Describe',3,'B'),('c','Calculate',2,'B'),('d','What',1,'C')]},
{'objectives':['05','06','06-03'],'prompt':'How far do you agree that this forest plan protects supply and habitats because its cutting is called selective? The hypothetical plan removes 100 wood units each interval while monitored regrowth is 60; other volume changes are stated absent. It records no retained-habitat or species-regeneration evidence. Judge sustained supply and the broader habitat claim. [5]','answer':'Disagree with both claims being established. Stock declines by 40 units per interval, contradicting maintained wood supply under the simplified balance. The selective label provides no missing regeneration or retained-habitat evidence. Lower extraction and monitoring of recovery and relevant ecological functions are needed; total volume alone would not prove habitat protection even if its balance were improved.','guide':['Up to 4 C marks for judgement using the stock balance, the label-versus-performance distinction and the missing broader-function evidence.','1 C mark for a supported overall decision addressing supply and habitat rather than a generic management list.'],'split':{'C':5},'parts':[('a','How far do you agree',5,'C')]}
]
